err and it's a theorem that, you know, that you're not going to be able to finish.

00:05:46.229 --> 00:05:51.148
So if I draw this as a graph, everybody on the first side is adjacent to everybody on the second side.

00:05:51.149 --> 00:05:52.199
And this is called K3,3

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:57.838
By the way, this guy's called K5. So K5 and K3,3 are two graphs that do not have planar embeddings.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:01.919
They're the two smallest graphs that don't have planar bearings. Okay. K somewhat

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:08.009
mystifyingly stands for complete, and so this is the complete graph on 5 vertices.

00:06:08.979 --> 00:06:12.508
Now this is not the complete graph, because obviously there are some pairs that are non-adjacent.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:18.089
You have two sides, this side and that side and everybody on this side is adjacent to everybody on that side.

00:06:18.129 --> 00:06:23.489
That's called the complete bipartite graph. And the way we know that it's complete bipartite and not complete is

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:29.460
because we have two numbers instead of one number. So Ka,b is a here b there, all adjacent to each other.

00:06:30.069 --> 00:06:32.729
Kt is t, all pairwise adjacent.

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:35.098
(Brady: Is there a way to tell what's embeddable and what's not?)

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.900
So there is a way to tell. And actually

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:40.590
it's a lovely theorem, it's a theorem I can tell you. You believed me that we can't embed those.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.590
In fact, maybe you shouldn't believe me but

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:46.739
there is an elegant proof of it. That you - you need to know a little bit of geometry,

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.229
you need to know something called Euler's formula and from that

00:06:49.229 --> 00:06:54.899
it's actually very easy to see that these two graphs are not embeddable. But let's say we believe that they're not embeddable.

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:56.190
So then here's another graph

00:06:56.190 --> 00:07:00.690
that's not embeddable. If I take an edge here and I, what we call, subdivide it.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:04.949
Which means I'll draw some vertices in the middle. That's another graph that's not embeddable, right?

00:07:04.949 --> 00:07:10.499
Because if I could embed this, now just delete vertices and so and you can do that for any edge you like here, you can do there.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:16.649
So these are all not embeddable, and they're called subdivisions. So we know that K5 is not embeddable,

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:25.289
K3,3 is not embeddable. And we also convinced ourselves that subdivisions of them - they're all not embeddable. And the theorem is,

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:32.880
that's it. The only reason some graph is not embeddable is because there's one of those bad guys sitting in it. You can

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:39.800
delete vertices and edges from it and find either a subdivision of K3,3 or a subdivision of K5. It's called Kuratowski's theorem.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:43.500
(Brady: Just those two?)
- Just those two. It's a miracle!

00:07:44.169 --> 00:07:48.000
(Brady: So though - they're like the two spanners in the works?)

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.909
Exactly. So the theorem is: G is planar if - if and only if, G has

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:56.729
no

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:57.970
K5

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:00.690
subdivision and no K3,3.

00:08:01.389 --> 00:08:02.470
(Brady: Wow!)

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:09.539
(So every single graph - if I drew you the most complicated graph in the world, and it was non embeddable)

00:08:09.849 --> 00:08:15.449
(you'd be able to find that in there somewhere?)
- That is correct. That's the only reason something is not embeddable.

00:08:15.449 --> 00:08:19.199
So these graphs they're very special. What - most graphs are not like that. It's a very

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.289
special kind of graphs that are embeddable.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:28.590
It's not that other graphs - that other graphs are bad, other graphs are good. In fact, most of the work is with other graphs.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:34.320
It's just if you work with those, you can suddenly prove much stronger theorems. It's like

00:08:36.459 --> 00:08:39.569
you know, you can think of all numbers or you can think of powers of

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:42.419
2. 
- (Brady: Or prime - ) 
- Prime numbers

00:08:42.849 --> 00:08:47.369
Powers of 2 are much nicer than normal numbers. It's not that the other numbers are not good,

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:51.390
it's just you know - in some way the other numbers are more interesting because they're not powers of two.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:52.920
But there are certain theorems

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:58.199
you can only prove about powers of two. So probably the most famous theory about - theorem about planar graphs is that

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:02.550
you can colour them with four colours. So maybe I'll talk about a little bit?

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:10.049
So colouring graphs means you want to colour the vertices so that adjacent vertices get different colours.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:15.750
So, for example in this graph I can colour this blue, and this green,

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:25.019
and also this green, this red, and just like this blue and, and this is a colouring. This is a colouring with three colours. So

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:32.159
adjacent pairs get different colours; this is colouring with three colours. And you can see that you couldn't do it with fewer colours than three,

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:37.019
because you have these three vertices all pairwise adjacent. So you have to use three.

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:43.529
And now the question is if I give you a graph what the smallest number of colours you can use to colour it?

00:09:43.529 --> 00:09:48.719
So one thing you immediately notice is that if you have a bunch of vertices all pairwise adjacent then

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:54.329
you need at least that many colours. In fact sometimes we need many more. This is a whole different

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:56.970
branch of math that's very active at the moment, but

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:04.259
often you may need many more but at least this is an obvious lower bound. Now, in planar graphs

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:10.290
we know that you don't have a K5. So you're not gonna have five vertices all pairwise adjacent.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:15.840
So at least this naive bound only would give you, that you need four. And the theorem is four is enough.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:20.879
The theorem is, it's called the four colour theorem. Every planar graph is four-colourable. It comes from map colouring.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:24.120
It was actually conjectured by, by a

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:26.740
cartographer in, in

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:33.299
eighteen something 1863 I think. Where he said if I have a map and I want to colour the countries so that

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.880
adjacent countries get different colours,

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:40.589
how many colours do I need? I seem to be noticing that four is always enough. And uhh,

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:45.389
and the answer is yes, four is always enough, because you can easily

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:50.820
translate colouring a map into colouring a planar graph. What you do is take a map,

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:56.729
alright, so the countries are this outside ring and then those triangles. So now I'm going to make a graph out of it.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:11:00.629
I'm going to put a vertex in the middle of each country. If two countries share the border

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:03.639
I'm going to put an edge between the two vertices. Another way to think about it

00:11:03.639 --> 00:11:08.439
I'm kind of, turning the edges. This edge used to go like this between these two countries, but now I'm gonna put it

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:13.029
perpendicularly. So because this is a mess let me just draw the blue graph again,

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:17.259
so this is already better, but now I can draw it even better. You can do that.

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:21.909
So anyway, so the black thing there was a map. The blue thing

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:25.480
here is a planar graph. If I colour the vertices of this planar graph and

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:30.909
then I use these colours to colour the regions of this map, the countries of this map,

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:33.819
it's it's the same problem. And so now the theorem is that

00:11:34.579 --> 00:11:37.779
it's enough to use four colours. For all of this to be true

00:11:37.779 --> 00:11:45.189
you need some slightly technical assumptions. Like in this map, you can't have Alaska. The countries have to be contiguous or as in math we say

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:46.970
connected. Because it's very easy to construct

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:52.990
a map where each country is a hundred islands and you need a hundred colours to colour it, but that's not what we mean.

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:58.810
What we mean is we only - all countries are connected. 
